bibliograph-worker (0.2.14-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.14 release: ISBNdb as a metered genre source and a soft quota gauge.

    - `catalog.genres` now falls back to ISBNdb when Goodreads and OpenLibrary
      both return nothing: `genresForEdition` reads the ISBN's book record and
      its `subjects` list, which ISBNdb marks deprecated, so it is a last
      resort and not a primary source. It goes through the provider's metered
      path — a cached `/book/{isbn}` (seven days) costs no daily call, a miss
      reserves one from `provider_daily_usage` — and a refused quota answers
      `undefined`, so the ledger row is left unmarked instead of recording "no
      genres". `mergeGenres` appends the subject sources alphabetically after
      Goodreads' relevance order, and 0006-genre-sources.sql widens the
      `edition_genres.source` CHECK to accept `isbndb`.
    - The Prometheus exposition splits the ISBNdb quota into a hard limit and a
      soft one: `bibliograph_isbndb_daily_limit` (5000, what `reserveDailyCall`
      enforces) and `bibliograph_isbndb_daily_soft_limit` (95% of it — 4750 —
      where an alert should fire), with `bibliograph_isbndb_calls_remaining`
      and `bibliograph_isbndb_calls_remaining_soft` alongside them.
      `renderMetrics` now defaults the limit instead of rendering 0.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:53:18 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.13-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.13 release: contributor merge support and a cheaper genre sweep.

    - One contributor (or publisher) can legitimately hold two credits on one
      edition — author and translator. `pds_enqueue_contributor_editions` and
      `pds_enqueue_publisher_editions` (0001) joined the link table by entity id
      and inserted one outbox row per credit position, so the second insert
      made `ON CONFLICT` fail with `cannot affect row a second time` (21000) and
      rolled back the whole edit. Both now select `DISTINCT` editions. The web
      editor's new contributor merge creates exactly that state, which is how
      the defect surfaced; the definitions are `CREATE OR REPLACE`, so applying
      the schema repairs an existing database.
    - `catalog.genres.sweep` no longer proves an empty fresh set with a set
      difference over the whole catalog: two index-only counts short-circuit it
      when every edition already has a ledger row. The retry pass reads the
      ledger directly and orders by its clock, and
      `edition_genre_fetches_retry_idx` (0005) is partial on
      `outcome IS DISTINCT FROM 'found'` so already-answered editions leave the
      walk, replacing the per-row join back to `edition_genres`.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 08:31:35 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.12-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.12 release: contributor biographies and edition genres.

    - Persist the provider biography of each credited contributor on ingest.
      OpenLibrary author detail is now read for every keyed author of an
      edition lookup — the name and the biography — still bounded to ten per
      lookup and shared across a work's editions. The new
      `describeContributors` provider method fills a missing bio for the admin
      transport. `linkContributors` writes `contributors.description`, clipped
      to the record's 1024-grapheme / 8192-byte lexicon bounds, and fills it
      only when the row has none, so an operator-curated bio is never
      replaced. `/ingest` and `/publisher-import` enrich before writing;
      `catalog.search` is unchanged, so a 50-candidate run cannot multiply
      upstream calls. No backfill: existing rows gain a bio the next time an
      edition crediting them is ingested.
    - Add edition genres (0005-genres.sql): a curated `genres` vocabulary, the
      `edition_genres` links and an `edition_genre_fetches` claim ledger, plus
      `catalog_enqueue_genre_fetch` as the single enqueue point shared by the
      edge and the worker. The new `catalog.genres` task hydrates one edition
      from OpenLibrary subjects and Goodreads book-page genres, mapped to
      canonical names before storage; `catalog.genres.sweep` walks ungenred
      editions every ten minutes and once at worker boot. A label with no
      canonical mapping is dropped rather than stored raw. Genres are
      edge-only and are not part of the published edition record.
    - Provider reads: OpenLibrary `subjectsFromWork` (with one redirect hop
      followed for a merged work key) and Goodreads `genresForEdition`, which
      resolves by Goodreads id or ISBN and never falls back to a title search.
      A WAF challenge still propagates as a provider failure, not an empty
      answer.

